**A model answer.** The test in `tests/practice-broken.ts`:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('the login page shows the Login button', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://test-automation-banking.vercel.app/');
  await expect(page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Login' })).toBeVisible();
});
```

The notes in the template:

```ts
// Total before the rename: Total: 6 tests in 1 file
//
// Total after the rename: Total: 9 tests in 2 files
//
// Why the total changed:
//   Playwright runs only files whose names end in .spec.ts or .test.ts.
//   After the rename, it found the file and listed its test once for
//   each of the three browsers.
```

**Why this problem matters.** Most mistakes in a test show up as a failure. A wrongly named file shows up as nothing, and every run still passes. Running `npx playwright test --list` shows what Playwright can actually see.
